#1. What do all the methods (move(), special_move(), use()) in the previous activities have in
#common?
# - All these methods represent actions that different objects can perform. They allow different classes to use the same method name while having different implementations or behaviors.
#2. How does this activity show polymorphism in a gaming context?
# - This shows how different game characters or items can have the same action (method) but behave differently based on their specific class. For example, the move() method may result in different movements for different characters, and the use() method may restore different amounts of health or mana depending on the item being used.
#3. Can you think of other examples where the same action behaves differently depending on
#the character or item?
# - For example in animals, when they are tasked to make a sound they may respond differently. A dog may bark, a cat may meow, and a bird may chirp. In a game context, the attack() method could result in different attack styles or damage outputs depending on whether the character is a warrior, mage, or archer.